 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Hysteresis",
   "expr": "ΔVT = VT+ – VT–",
   "where": {
    "VT+": "positive-going input threshold voltage",
    "VT–": "negative-going input threshold voltage"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 5
  },
  {
   "name": "Inverter function",
   "expr": "Y = NOT A",
   "where": {
    "A": "gate input (1A or 2A)",
    "Y": "gate output (1Y or 2Y)"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 1
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Two separate inverters in one package, each giving Y = NOT A: a high input gives a low output and a low input a high output."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Schmitt-trigger inputs",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Each input switches high at the positive-going threshold VT+ and back low at the lower negative-going threshold VT–, so slow or noisy edges come out clean. Hysteresis is 0.4 V to 1.1 V at VCC = 3 V and 0.7 V to 1.4 V at 5.5 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Partial power down (Ioff)",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "With VCC at 0 V the outputs are switched off, so no damaging current flows back through the part from a driven input or output; leakage is at most ±10 μA with 5.5 V applied. Boards can therefore be hot-plugged, sections powered off, and the part back-driven without harm."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input tolerance and down translation",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The inputs take up to 5.5 V at any supply from 1.65 V to 5.5 V. Once the input rises past VT+ max the output goes to the VCC level, so a 5 V signal comes out at 3.3 V and a 3.3 V signal at 1.8 V when VCC is set to the lower level."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output drive",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "CMOS output with balanced source and sink drive: ±24 mA at 3.3 V, ±32 mA at 4.5 V. Load current stays within IO max (±50 mA) per output and within ±100 mA through VCC or GND for the whole part; outputs are not pulled above VCC. In bus contention the output can drive currents past the maximum ratings, and into a light load its fast edges can ring."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output noise",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "At VCC = 3.3 V and 25 °C, typical output ground bounce VOLP is below 0.8 V and typical VOH undershoot VOHV stays above 2 V. Maximum tpd is 5.4 ns at 3.3 V; ICC is 10 μA max."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply and bypassing",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Supply: any voltage within the 1.65 V to 5.5 V rating. Bypass: 0.1 μF on a part with one VCC pin; 0.01 μF or 0.022 μF per pin on a part with several. Capacitors of different values in parallel cover different noise frequencies; 0.1 μF with 1 μF is a common pair. The capacitor goes as near the VCC pin as possible."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unused inputs and layout",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "No input may float: an unused input is tied to VCC or GND, whichever suits the function, since an open CMOS input has no defined level. Signal traces are kept short."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Latch-up and ESD",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Latch-up performance passes 100 mA per JESD 78, Class II. ESD: ±2000 V HBM, ±1000 V CDM. Built-in ESD protection is limited; the leads are shorted together or the part is stored and handled on conductive foam."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Automotive version",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "An automotive version qualified to AEC-Q100 is SN74LVC2G14-Q1."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packages",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "All packages are MSL level 1 at 260 °C peak reflow. DCK (SC70) matches JEDEC MO-203, variation AB, with mold flash and protrusion up to 0.15 mm per side outside the body figures. DBV (SOT-23) references JEDEC MO-178, with mold flash up to 0.25 mm per side, and leads 1, 2 and 3 can be made broader than leads 4, 5 and 6 to show orientation. YZP is the NanoFree package: the silicon die, with solder balls on it, serves as the package."
  }
 ],
